               Evan and Neves, Maria Inês and Ng, Ding Quan and Obene, Tontel
               and Pera, Victor and Pratt, Nicole and Rao, Gowtham and
               Rappoport, Nadav and Reinecke, Ines and Saroufim, Paola and
               Shoaibi, Azza and Simon, Katherine and Suchard, Marc A and
               Swerdel, Joel N and Voss, Erica A and Weaver, James and Zhang,
               Linying and Hripcsak, George and Ryan, Patrick B",
  journal   = "J. Am. Med. Inform. Assoc.",
  publisher = "Oxford University Press (OUP)",
  volume    =  30,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "859--868",
  abstract  = "OBJECTIVE: Observational studies can impact patient care but must
               be robust and reproducible. Nonreproducibility is primarily
               caused by unclear reporting of design choices and analytic
               procedures. This study aimed to: (1) assess how the study logic
               described in an observational study could be interpreted by
               independent researchers and (2) quantify the impact of
               interpretations' variability on patient characteristics.
               MATERIALS AND METHODS: Nine teams of highly qualified researchers
               reproduced a cohort from a study by Albogami et al. The teams
               were provided the clinical codes and access to the tools to
               create cohort definitions such that the only variable part was
               their logic choices. We executed teams' cohort definitions
               against the database and compared the number of subjects, patient
               overlap, and patient characteristics. RESULTS: On average, the
               teams' interpretations fully aligned with the master
               implementation in 4 out of 10 inclusion criteria with at least 4
               deviations per team. Cohorts' size varied from one-third of the
               master cohort size to 10 times the cohort size (2159-63 619
               subjects compared to 6196 subjects). Median agreement was 9.4\%
               (interquartile range 15.3-16.2\%). The teams' cohorts
               significantly differed from the master implementation by at least
               2 baseline characteristics, and most of the teams differed by at
               least 5. CONCLUSIONS: Independent research teams attempting to
               reproduce the study based on its free-text description alone
               produce different implementations that vary in the population
               size and composition. Sharing analytical code supported by a
               common data model and open-source tools allows reproducing a
               study unambiguously thereby preserving initial design choices.",
  month     =  "19~" # apr,
  year      =  2023,
  url       = "http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocad009",
  file      = "All Papers/2023/Ostropolets et al. 2023 - Reproducible variability - assessing investiga ... 9 research teams attempting to reproduce the same observational study.pdf",
  keywords  = "credibility; observational data; open science;
               reproducibility;TYPE-ME",
  doi       = "10.1093/jamia/ocad009",
  pmc       = "PMC10114120",
  pmid      =  36826399,
  issn      = "1527-974X,1067-5027",
  language  = "en"
}

